 To monitor the battery level of a Texas Instruments SensorTag, you'll need to use its Battery Voltage sensor (BVC) and interact with it using code in a microcontroller or software development kit (SDK). Here's a general outline of steps you can follow:

1. Connect the SensorTag to your computer or microcontroller. If you're using a microcontroller, make sure to program it with the correct SDK for the SensorTag.

2. Initialize the Battery Voltage sensor in your code, depending on the programming language and development environment you're using. For instance, if you're using the CC2650STK LaunchXL, and programming with TI's SimpleLink SDK, you can initialize the BVC as follows:

```c
ti_sensor_handle batterySensorHandle;
ti_error error = ti_sensor_init(boardObj, SENSOR_BATTERY_VOLTAGE, &batterySensorHandle);
if (error != TI_OK) {
    printf("Error initializing Battery Voltage sensor: %d\n", error);
}
```

3. After initialization, read the battery voltage data using `ti_sensor_invoke()` or similar function provided by your SDK. You can then use this value to estimate the battery level.

4. Finally, you can display the battery level in a user-friendly format on an integrated development environment (IDE), connected display, or even a mobile app if you're using Bluetooth Low Energy connectivity.

For further details and specific code snippets tailored to your setup, refer to Texas Instruments' official documentation for the SensorTag: https://www.ti.com/documentation/cc2650-sensortag.html
